 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections the project runs itself. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were the alternatives. Polling is the simplest, stateless operation but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag. A hosted service is the quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection operations, but carries recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting was chosen to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor, accepting the extra connection-capacity and reconnect operations as our own.
